  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Put the trigonometric functions together",
      "body": "<p>Try dividing by cos(2x) to make tangent. Before doing so, check in the original equation whether cos(2x)=0 or x=0 could give a solution.</p>",
      "recap": "Check a possible zero before dividing by an expression.",
      "pitfall": "A division can lose a solution if its divisor is zero.",
      "pause": "Rearrange to compare a tangent graph with a reciprocal graph.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b2-l04",
          "reminder": "Check a possible zero before dividing by an expression."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Mark the tangent graph carefully",
      "body": "<p>Mark the zeros and vertical asymptotes of the tangent curve over the stated interval. The factor 2 halves the usual spacing.</p>",
      "recap": "An input multiplier changes a trigonometric graph’s period.",
      "pitfall": "Do not use the spacing for tan(x).",
      "pause": "Add the reciprocal graph and identify the parts where their signs agree.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l15",
          "reminder": "An input multiplier changes a trigonometric graph’s period."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Count crossings on each part",
      "body": "<p>On each part between asymptotes, compare which curve rises and which falls. Check their relative heights at both ends.</p>",
      "recap": "If one curve rises and the other falls, they can cross at most once on that part.",
      "pitfall": "Neither an asymptote nor an excluded input counts as a crossing.",
      "pause": "Check the original endpoints before giving your total.",
      "recall": []
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is not a solution. At a point where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>sin</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the original equality cannot hold. We may therefore rewrite it as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>The positive tangent branches in the allowed range are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Each rises continuously from 0 to infinity and meets the positive decreasing curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> exactly once. Negative tangent branches and the endpoints give no additional solutions.</p><p><strong>Answer E: 4 solutions.</strong></p>",
